# Without this file, what lands in a working tree is whatever the machine's
# `core.autocrlf` says, and this project is developed on Windows and built in
# CI on three platforms. For most files that difference is invisible. For two
# kinds it is not, and both were live faults before this file existed.

* text=auto

# refinery hashes the migration text a binary embeds at compile time, and
# refuses a database whose recorded hash no longer matches. With the text
# coming out of the checkout, the same commit produced binaries that hashed
# differently: the 1.0.0 Windows release, built on a runner that checked out
# CRLF, could not open a database created by the 1.0.0 Linux release or by any
# local build, and said so as `applied migration V1__projects_and_sessions is
# different than filesystem one`. The code normalises this now too — see
# `crates/anamnesis-store/src/migrations.rs` — because a file cannot reach
# every copy of these bytes. This line is the half that keeps them from
# diverging in the first place.
*.sql text eol=lf

# A CRLF shebang is not a shebang: the kernel reads `/bin/sh\r` as the
# interpreter's name and reports the script as missing.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows tooling and the MSVC linker are what these are for, and a CRLF
# batch file is the format they expect.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary, so that Git never rewrites a byte of them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.gz binary
*.zip binary
*.onnx binary
